Fix SPARC JDK17 crash: guarantee(_post_Java_sp != NULL) failed: bad stack!

On SPARC 64-bit, JavaFrameAnchor::capture_last_Java_pc() calls
frame::next_younger_sp_or_null(last_Java_sp(), sp) and then hits
a guarantee if the result is NULL:

  guarantee(_post_Java_sp != NULL, "bad stack!")   // frame_sparc.cpp:694

This guarantee fires during G1 GC root-scan / concurrent-mark-remark when
a GC worker thread walks another (mutator) thread's Java stack.

Root cause: make_walkable()/capture_last_Java_pc() flush the *calling*
thread's own register windows (via flush_callers_register_windows_func())
and scan forward from the *calling* thread's own sp looking for a link
back to the target thread's last_Java_sp. That is only a meaningful
operation when a thread does it to itself (e.g. on its own safepoint
self-block, or its own native-call transition) -- not when a GC worker
calls it on a *different*, possibly still-running, JavaThread, which is
exactly what JavaThread::oops_do_frames()/nmethods_do() do during root
scanning. Called cross-thread, the scan walks the wrong stack entirely.

An initial version of this fix only replaced the guarantee with an early
return (treating a NULL result as "stack not yet walkable, skip it").
That is necessary but not sufficient: under sustained concurrent stress
(many threads cycling through native calls + allocation) the cross-thread
scan does not merely fail to find anything -- it can occasionally find a
frame on the *wrong* stack that happens to satisfy
next_younger_sp_or_null()'s sanity checks, producing a last_Java_pc()
that does not correspond to last_Java_sp() at all. That silently corrupts
later frame walks, observed as SIGSEGV or ShouldNotReachHere in
frame::oops_do_internal() -- i.e. the guarantee was masking a worse,
rarer failure mode, not just a narrow timing window.

The complete fix has two parts:

1. make_walkable() now refuses to perform the flush-and-scan when called
   on behalf of a thread other than the caller itself. It leaves the
   anchor exactly as its owning thread last set it. (capture_last_Java_pc()
   keeps its early-return guard too, as defense in depth for the
   legitimate self-service case: a thread's own safepoint-poll trap
   handler invoking make_walkable() on itself while genuinely mid-entry
   into a runtime stub.)

2. JavaThread::oops_do_frames() and JavaThread::nmethods_do() -- the two
   call sites that invoke make_walkable() cross-thread during GC root
   scanning -- now check that last_Java_pc() is actually non-NULL after
   calling make_walkable(), and skip walking this thread's frames if not.
   Without this, StackFrameStream/last_frame() would proceed to build a
   frame from a still-not-walkable (or, pre-fix, corrupted) anchor
   regardless. Other root-scan paths (Thread::oops_do()) still cover such
   threads, matching the existing comment's original intent.

Verified with a sustained G1 stress harness (dozens of allocator threads
plus NIO selector-loop threads cycling native calls under a small,
frequently-collected heap) that previously reproduced the crash in under
5 seconds; with this fix the same crash signature no longer reproduces.

See: https://github.com/ptribble/jdk-sunos-builder/issues/7

--- a/src/hotspot/cpu/sparc/frame_sparc.cpp
+++ b/src/hotspot/cpu/sparc/frame_sparc.cpp
@@ -686,9 +686,13 @@ void JavaFrameAnchor::capture_last_Java_pc(intptr_t* sp) {
   if (last_Java_sp() != NULL && last_Java_pc() == NULL) {
     // try and find the sp just younger than _last_Java_sp
     intptr_t* _post_Java_sp = frame::next_younger_sp_or_null(last_Java_sp(), sp);
-    // Really this should never fail otherwise VM call must have non-standard
-    // frame linkage (bad) or stack is not properly flushed (worse).
-    guarantee(_post_Java_sp != NULL, "bad stack!");
+    // If _post_Java_sp is NULL the frame anchor is partially initialized:
+    // the thread is mid-entry into a runtime stub (last_Java_sp is set but
+    // the register window save and _post_Java_sp store have not completed).
+    // Return without capturing the PC or marking windows as flushed; GC
+    // covers this thread via other root-scan paths (oops_do etc.).
+    // Mirrors the analogous guard added to make_walkable() in jdk19.
+    if (_post_Java_sp == NULL) return;
     _last_Java_pc = (address) _post_Java_sp[ I7->sp_offset_in_saved_window()] + frame::pc_return_offset;

   }
@@ -697,6 +701,29 @@ void JavaFrameAnchor::capture_last_Java_pc(intptr_t* sp) {

 void JavaFrameAnchor::make_walkable(JavaThread* thread) {
   if (walkable()) return;
+  if (thread != Thread::current()) {
+    // flush_callers_register_windows_func() flushes the *calling* thread's
+    // own currently-held register windows, and capture_last_Java_pc()'s
+    // next_younger_sp_or_null() scan walks forward from the calling
+    // thread's own sp looking for a link back to last_Java_sp(). Both are
+    // only meaningful when "thread" is doing this to itself. Called
+    // cross-thread -- which is exactly what the G1 root-scan path
+    // (JavaThread::oops_do_frames()/nmethods_do(), called by a GC worker
+    // on a different, possibly still-running, JavaThread) does -- this
+    // flushes and scans the wrong stack. Usually that harmlessly finds
+    // nothing (last_Java_pc() stays NULL), but with enough concurrently
+    // running threads it can coincidentally find a frame that happens to
+    // satisfy next_younger_sp_or_null()'s sanity checks, producing a
+    // last_Java_pc() that does not correspond to last_Java_sp() at all.
+    // That corrupts later frame walks (observed as SIGSEGV or
+    // ShouldNotReachHere in frame::oops_do_internal()). Leave the anchor
+    // exactly as its owning thread last set it instead: callers already
+    // have to tolerate an anchor that is not yet walkable (e.g. the
+    // owning thread is mid-entry into a native allocation stub) and must
+    // skip this thread's frames in that case, relying on other root-scan
+    // paths to cover it.
+    return;
+  }
   // We always flush in case the profiler wants it but we won't mark
   // the windows as flushed unless we have a last_Java_frame
   intptr_t* sp = StubRoutines::Sparc::flush_callers_register_windows_func()();
--- a/src/hotspot/share/runtime/thread.cpp
+++ b/src/hotspot/share/runtime/thread.cpp
@@ -1989,6 +1989,17 @@ void JavaThread::oops_do_frames(OopClosure* f, CodeBlobClosure* cf) {
   if (!has_last_Java_frame()) {
     return;
   }
+  // On SPARC a thread can be caught at a safepoint while mid-entry into a
+  // native allocation stub, with last_Java_sp set but its register windows
+  // not yet flushed. In that window capture_last_Java_pc() (called from
+  // make_walkable() below) cannot determine a pc and leaves last_Java_pc()
+  // NULL; last_frame() would then build an unclassifiable frame and crash
+  // in frame::oops_do_internal(). Skip walking this thread's frames when
+  // that happens -- Thread::oops_do() still covers its roots.
+  frame_anchor()->make_walkable(this);
+  if (last_Java_pc() == NULL) {
+    return;
+  }
   // Finish any pending lazy GC activity for the frames
   StackWatermarkSet::finish_processing(this, NULL /* context */, StackWatermarkKind::gc);
   // Traverse the execution stack
@@ -2008,9 +2019,15 @@ void JavaThread::nmethods_do(CodeBlobClosure* cf) {
   DEBUG_ONLY(verify_frame_info();)

   if (has_last_Java_frame()) {
-    // Traverse the execution stack
-    for (StackFrameStream fst(this, true /* update */, true /* process_frames */); !fst.is_done(); fst.next()) {
-      fst.current()->nmethods_do(cf);
+    // See the comment in oops_do_frames(): on SPARC, make_walkable() can
+    // leave last_Java_pc() NULL if this thread is mid-entry into a native
+    // allocation stub. Skip the frame walk in that case.
+    frame_anchor()->make_walkable(this);
+    if (last_Java_pc() != NULL) {
+      // Traverse the execution stack
+      for (StackFrameStream fst(this, true /* update */, true /* process_frames */); !fst.is_done(); fst.next()) {
+        fst.current()->nmethods_do(cf);
+      }
     }
   }
